WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:17.240
Pneumonia is inflammation in your lungs caused by an infection.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:22.730
You have two lungs, one on each side of your chest.

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:26.900
Each lung has separate sections, called
lobes.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:32.060
Normally, as you breathe, air moves freely through your trachea, or windpipe,

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:36.080
then through large tubes, called bronchi,

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.440
through smaller tubes, called bronchioles,

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:45.420
and finally into tiny sacs, called alveoli.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.900
Your airways and alveoli are flexible
and springy.

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:54.780
When you breathe in, each air sac inflates
like a small balloon.

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:58.710
And when you exhale, the sacs deflate.

00:00:59.309 --> 00:01:04.009
Small blood vessels, called capillaries, surround your alveoli.

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:09.540
Oxygen from the air you breathe passes into your capillaries,

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:13.880
then carbon dioxide from your body passes out of your capillaries

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:18.660
into your alveoli so that your lungs can get rid of it when you exhale.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:27.360
If you have pneumonia, your airways or lungs have an infection caused by germs

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:28.340
such as:

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:30.320
bacteria

00:01:31.229 --> 00:01:33.129
viruses

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:35.480
fungi

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:38.420
or parasites.

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:43.190
Your airways catch most germs

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:47.810
In the mucus that lines your trachea, bronchi, and bronchioles.

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:51.300
Hair-like cilia lining the tubes

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:55.170
constantly push the mucus and germs out of your airways,

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:58.010
where you may expel them by coughing.

00:01:58.909 --> 00:02:02.439
Sometimes germs make it past your mucus and cilia,

00:02:02.439 --> 00:02:04.419
and enter your alveoli.

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:10.690
Normally, cells of your immune system attack these germs,

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:12.990
which keep them from making you sick.

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:17.180
However, if your immune system is
weakened due to

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:19.240
age, illness, or fatigue,

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:21.580
pneumonia-causing germs can

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:25.220
overwhelm your immune cells and begin to multiply.

00:02:26.849 --> 00:02:30.319
Your bronchioles and alveoli become inflamed

00:02:30.319 --> 00:02:34.590
as your immune system attacks the
multiplying germs.

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:38.690
The inflammation causes your alveoli fill with fluid,

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:43.090
making it difficult for your body to get
the oxygen it needs.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.680
If you have lobar pneumonia, one lobe of your lungs is affected.

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:54.530
If you have bronchopneumonia, many areas of both lungs are affected.

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:58.130
Pneumonia may cause the following
symptoms:

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:00.130
difficulty breathing

00:03:00.569 --> 00:03:02.149
chest pain

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:06.030
coughing fever and chills

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:07.890
confusion

00:03:08.209 --> 00:03:09.489
headache

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:10.880
muscle pain

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:12.480
and fatigue.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:19.550
Pneumonia can lead to serious complications.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:23.950
Respiratory failure occurs when your breathing becomes so difficult

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:28.270
that you need a machine called a ventilator to help you breathe.

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:34.110
Bacteremia occurs when the bacteria
causing your pneumonia

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:37.460
move into your bloodstream where they
may travel to infect

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:38.640
other organs.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:43.210
In some cases of pneumonia,

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:50.030
a large collection of fluid and pus, called an abscess, may form inside one of your lungs.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.920
If an abscess forms around the outside of your lung, it’s called an empyema.

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:02.020
Possible treatments for pneumonia
include

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:06.460
antibiotics, if the cause is bacteria or a parasite

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:11.700
antiviral drugs, if the cause is a flu virus

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:16.900
antifungal medication, if the cause is a
fungus

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:21.340
rest and drinking plenty of fluids

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:25.780
and over-the-counter, or OTC remedies,

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:28.680
to manage your fever, aches, and pain.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:33.560
If you have severe pneumonia you may be
admitted to the hospital,

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:37.300
and given intravenous antibiotics and
oxygen.

